A||||8||BOILERMAKER||Productive time on my GT||||1
Z||000000||BOILERMAKER||10-11-1999||09:40 AM||||Boy did I have a hay-day with my GT over the weekend and I had to share it with you guys (and gals).<P>I changed my aluminum clutch pedal to a steel one and boy what a difference.  The aluminum one must have been extremely bent (but not to the point of not engaging).  It's a completely different feel and wasn't all that hard.  It took some instructions from V8 Archies Archisms and a little thinking about the best way to do things.  As far as inconvenience I would say that this was one of the most uncomfortable positions to be in, but the work wasn't hard at all.<P>I changed my trunk gasket (the previous one was pretty much shot.<P>I lubricated each door's locking mechanisms.  Previously the electric locks wouldn't travel all the way and I had to hit the button several times to get them to fully travel.  Now they work as if they were new.  Along with this I also installed the keyless entry and boy am I happy.<P>I also changed my IAC.  The hesitation is still there, but everything is getting better.  I am in the process of checking all lines to make sure there aren't any cracks or such.  I think this may be one of my biggest problems.<P>Now all I need to do is paint this baby in the spring and I will be sitting pretty.||||reg||1
Z||000001||88 Fiero||10-11-1999||10:26 AM||||Boiler...have you thought of cleaning out the throttle body of your car...You will be surprise on how much gunk is in there also when you take out your IAC spray a cleaner inside and a small rag to clean it too...that can cause your hesitation too much gunk ....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bill Osmer||10-11-1999||01:37 PM||||Maybe a new thread is needed but...<P>When you lubricated your doors' locking mechanisms, did you have any particular procedure to follow? Or did you have the doors disassembled (for the RKE) and you had "easy" access. Did you use white lith lube or ???||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||10-11-1999||01:51 PM||||I mainly just took off the door inside panel and lubricated everything that moved with PB Blaster.  I like that stuff more than the WD-40.  <P>I even squirted the joints in the electric window and it works much better now also.  Amazing what a little care will do for a car.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DJRice||10-12-1999||09:31 AM||||About 3 weeks ago I removed my IAC and cleaned it and what I could get to in the Throttle Body.  It was a bit of work and at first it did not seem to do much good, however, after about a week, I noticed that my car had finally started to idle much more smoothly and took a whole lot less time to 'warm up.'<P>Chances are that if you cleaned it well, that in a few days you will be pleased with the results...||||reg||1
Z||000005||BOILERMAKER||10-12-1999||10:20 AM||||I've already changed my IAC out and before that I took out the old IAC and cleaned really well.  There was very very little gunk in there if any.  I looked over all the lines last night and nothing seems to be amiss.  Boy, this little annoyance is very frustrating.  More work to do and little time to do it.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bruce||10-12-1999||10:21 PM||||Boilermaker,<BR>Where did you get your new clutch pedal and bushings? Do you remember the part #? I think my clutch pedal needs replacing, too.<BR>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000007||BOILERMAKER||10-13-1999||08:55 AM||||For a part number on a clutch pedal, check out V8 archies archisms.<P> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com/arch6.htm</A> <P>I believe this has the part # on it.  I had an 87 GT already parted out and used the clutch pedal from that.  Keep in mind that this archism says that the spring is useless and should be thrown away.  I re-used the old spring and it works like a charm.  <P>Since I didn't order the parts from the dealer, I didn't have any of the extras and had to use all the original parts, but it worked and it is sweet now.||||reg||1
Z||000008||BOILERMAKER||10-17-1999||07:44 PM||||OK, replaced the MAP sensor and also the tach filter (had one laying around and decided to see if it made a difference).  I've already replaced EGR, IAC, TPS (year or so ago) and O2 sensor (year or so ago).  Hesitation is still there.<P>Thinking that next step (O boy) would be too look into the air intake gaskets and replace them.  Sounds logical?  Also thought about that throttle body/butterfly valve that the throttle cable hooks to.  Would this cause the symptoms that I am experiencing? <P>I have already checked all tubes and nothing seems to be cracked.  I have bended and twisted and nothing is cracked.  This V6 has come from another fiero and it had the same problem in that one.  So I am positive that the problem is in the engine somewhere.  <P>All it is is an annoyance, but as time passes on and I am getting closer to the new paint job, I want the engine to run like the car looks.<P>Help me brainstorm guys.  I don't want to replace gaskets (timely job, but not extremely tough), but I will if it comes down to it.||||reg||1
